QUESTIONS=[
 r'Find critical values and show where each function increases/decreases: (a) f(x) = x³ + 3x² + 1; (b) f(x) = x³ + 35x² − 125x − 9.375.',
 r'Find critical values: (a) f(x) = 2x³ − 3x² − 72x + 15; (b) \(f(x)=\frac13x^3-x^2-15x+6\); (c) \(f(x)=6x^{2/3}-4x\); (d) \(f(x)=3x^{4/3}-12x^{1/3}\).',
 r'Determine relative maximum, minimum or neither at the given critical values: (a) f(x) = (x³ − 3x + 1)⁷ = 0 at x = 1,x = −1; (b) f(x) = (x⁴ − 4x + 2)⁵ at x = 1; (c) f(x) = (x² − 4)⁴(x² − 1)³ at x = 1,x = 2; (d) f(x) = ∛(x³ − 48) at x = 4.',
 r'Find all critical points, determine rising/falling and concave up/down intervals, and sketch: (a) f(x) = 2(x + 20)² − 8(x + 20) + 7; (b) \(f(x)=\frac13x^3-9x+2\).',
 r'Find all relative extrema: (a) f(x) = x³ − 3x² + 1; (b) f(x) = x³ + 6x² + 9x + 2.',
 r'(a) Suppose f(x) is a differential function with derivative f′(x) = (x − 1)²(x − 2)(x − 4)(x + 5)⁴. Find all critical values and classify each as a relative maximum, minimum or neither. (b) Suppose f(x) is a differential function with derivative \(f′(x)=\frac{(2x-1)(x+3)}{(x-1)^2}\). Find all critical values and classify each as maximum, minimum or neither.',
 r'A company has found through experience that increasing its advertising also increases its sales up to a point. The company believes that the mathematical model connecting profit in hundreds of dollars P(x) and expenditures on advertising in thousands of dollars x is: P(x) = 80 + 108x − x³, 0 ≤ x ≤ 10. (a) Find the advertising expenditure leading to maximum profit. (b) Find the maximum profit.',
 r'The total profit P(x) in thousands of dollars from the sale of x hundred thousands of automobile tires is approximated by P(x) = −x³ + 9x² + 120x − 400, 3 ≤ x ≤ 15. Find the number of hundred thousands of tires that must be sold to maximize profit. Find the maximum profit.',
 r'The percent of concentration of a drug in the bloodstream x hours after administration is \(K(x)=\frac{4x}{3x^2+27}\). (a) On what time intervals is the concentration increasing? (b) On what intervals is it decreasing? (c) Find when concentration is a maximum. (d) Find maximum concentration.',
 r'A diesel generator burns fuel at \(G(x)=\frac1{48}\left(\frac{300}{x}+2x\right)\) gallons/hour when producing x thousand kilowatt hours of electricity. Fuel costs $2.25/gallon, and the generator operates for 32 hours. Find x leading to minimum total cost, and find that minimum cost.'
]
STEPS=[
 [r'(a) Differentiate: f′ = 3x² + 6x = 3x(x + 2). Zeros are −2 and 0.',r'The derivative is positive for x < −2 and x > 0, and negative for −2 < x < 0. Thus f increases on (−∞,−2),(0,∞) and decreases on (−2,0).',r'The corresponding points are (−2,5) and (0,1); the first is a relative maximum and the second a minimum.',r'(b) f′ = 3x² + 70x − 125. Its discriminant is 70² + 4·3·125 = 6400 = 80².',r'Roots are (−70 ± 80)/6, namely −25 and 5/3; f′ = 3(x + 25)(x − 5/3).',r'The upward quadratic is positive outside these roots and negative between them, so f increases on (−∞,−25),(5/3,∞) and decreases on (−25,5/3).',r'The key’s extra extrema-coordinate statements for part (b) disagree with this source polynomial; the interval calculation follows the independently factored derivative.'],
 [r'All four real source functions are defined at zero, including the cube-root powers; a critical value has zero derivative or no derivative at a defined point.',r'(a) f′ = 6x² − 6x − 72 = 6(x − 4)(x + 3), giving −3 and 4.',r'(b) f′ = x² − 2x − 15 = (x − 5)(x + 3), giving −3 and 5.',r'(c) For x ≠ 0, f′ = 4/∛x − 4. It is zero when ∛x = 1, so x = 1.',r'At 0, 6x²/³ − 4x is defined but its derivative fails to exist; include 0 as well as 1.',r'(d) For x ≠ 0, f′ = (4x − 4)/(∛x)². Its only zero is 1.',r'The function is defined but nondifferentiable at 0, so part (d) also has critical values 0 and 1.'],
 [r'(a) The printed “= 0” is inconsistent with its listed points: (1³ − 3·1 + 1)⁷ = −1 and ((−1)³ − 3(−1) + 1)⁷ = 2187, not zero. Classify the stated function rather than treating that extra equality as a true value.',r'For f = (x³ − 3x + 1)⁷, f′ = 21(x² − 1)(x³ − 3x + 1)⁶. Near −1 the sign changes + to −, giving a maximum at (−1,2187); near 1 it changes − to +, giving a minimum at (1,−1).',r'(b) Put u = x⁴ − 4x + 2. At 1, u = −1,u′ = 0,u″ = 12. For u⁵, f″(1) = 5u⁴u″ = 60 > 0, giving a relative minimum f(1) = −1.',r'(c) Near 1, (x² − 4)⁴ is positive and (x² − 1)³ changes sign from negative to positive. Its cubic-order crossing is a stationary inflection, not an extremum; f(1) = 0.',r'Near 2, (x² − 1)³ is positive and (x² − 4)⁴ is nonnegative with a zero at 2, so f(2) = 0 is a relative minimum.',r'(d) For the printed f = ∛(x³ − 48), f′ = \(\frac{x^2}{(x^3-48)^{2/3}}\) where x³ ≠ 48. At x = 4, f′ = 16/16²/³ > 0, so 4 is not even a critical value and is neither maximum nor minimum.',r'The key’s minimum at 4 would instead fit the different function ∛(x³ − 48x), whose derivative changes − to + at 4. That missing-x alternative is not silently substituted into the source question.',r'The printed cube-root function actually has critical values 0 and ∛48: 0 has zero derivative without an extremum, and ∛48 has an undefined finite derivative without an extremum. It is strictly increasing overall.'],
 [r'(a) Complete the square: f = 2(x + 18)² − 1. Its derivative is 4x + 72 = 4(x + 18), and f″ = 4.',r'The only critical point is (−18,−1), a relative and global minimum.',r'The graph falls for x < −18 and rises for x > −18. It is concave up everywhere and never concave down; no inflection exists.',r'The source-specific sketch is an upward parabola with this vertex; both branches extend without bound.',r'(b) f′ = x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3); f″ = 2x.',r'The critical points are (−3,20), a relative maximum, and (3,−16), a relative minimum.',r'The function rises on (−∞,−3),(3,∞), and falls on (−3,3). It is concave down for x < 0 and concave up for x > 0.',r'The inflection point is (0,2). The dedicated cubic sketch marks both extrema and the change of concavity.'],
 [r'(a) f′ = 3x² − 6x = 3x(x − 2), so critical values are 0 and 2.',r'f″ = 6x − 6, giving f″(0) = −6 < 0 and f″(2) = 6 > 0.',r'Thus relative maximum is (0,1) and relative minimum is (2,−3).',r'(b) f′ = 3x² + 12x + 9 = 3(x + 1)(x + 3), so critical values are −3 and −1.',r'The second derivative is 6x + 12: −6 at −3 and 6 at −1.',r'Thus relative maximum is (−3,2), relative minimum (−1,−2).',r'Each cubic is defined and differentiable everywhere, so there are no additional critical values from derivative failure.'],
 [r'(a) The derivative zeros are −5,1,2,4. The powers (x + 5)⁴ and (x − 1)² are even, so they do not change derivative sign when crossed.',r'Away from its zeros, the sign is that of (x − 2)(x − 4): positive for x < 2, negative for 2 < x < 4, positive for x > 4.',r'Therefore 2 is a relative maximum, 4 a relative minimum, and −5,1 are neither.',r'Function values are not supplied, so heights of these extrema cannot be determined from the derivative alone without an integration constant.',r'(b) The denominator is positive for x ≠ 1. Derivative zeros are −3 and 1/2.',r'For x < −3 the numerator is positive; for −3 < x < 1/2 it is negative; for x > 1/2 it is positive, separately on either side of 1.',r'The sign changes + to − at −3, giving a maximum; − to + at 1/2 gives a minimum.',r'The supplied derivative is undefined at 1 and behaves as 4/(x − 1)² there, so any antiderivative diverges near 1. No finite differentiable extension at 1 is possible. Treat 1 as outside the stated differentiable domain, not a critical value or a “neither” extremum.'],
 [r'Profit P is in hundreds of dollars, while x is advertising in thousands of dollars. Keep the two unit scales separate.',r'Differentiate: P′ = 108 − 3x². The zeros are ±6, but only 6 lies in [0,10].',r'For 0 ≤ x < 6, P′ > 0; for 6 < x ≤ 10, P′ < 0. The interior maximum is at x = 6.',r'Check the closed-interval candidates: P(0) = 80, P(6) = 512, P(10) = 160.',r'The largest value is 512 hundreds of dollars, or $51,200.',r'The advertising expenditure x = 6 means $6,000, not $600.',r'The second derivative P″(6) = −36 provides a local maximum check; endpoint comparison establishes the maximum on the whole source interval.'],
 [r'Here x counts hundred thousands of tires and P counts thousands of dollars, with 3 ≤ x ≤ 15.',r'P′ = −3x² + 18x + 120 = −3(x − 10)(x + 4).',r'The only derivative zero in the source interval is x = 10; −4 is excluded.',r'Check P(3) = 14, P(10) = 700 and P(15) = 50, all in thousands of dollars.',r'The greatest is 700 at x = 10. The derivative changes from positive to negative there.',r'Ten hundred thousands of tires is 1,000,000 tires.',r'The maximum profit is $700,000. The second derivative −6x + 18 is −42 at x = 10, confirming a local maximum.'],
 [r'Use physical time x ≥ 0. K(x) is expressed as a percent, not as a dimensionless fraction.',r'Quotient rule gives K′ = [4(3x² + 27) − 24x²]/(3x² + 27)² = 4(9 − x²)/[3(x² + 9)²].',r'The denominator is positive. For 0 < x < 3 the numerator is positive, so concentration increases.',r'For x > 3 the numerator is negative, so it decreases.',r'The maximum occurs at x = 3 hours: K(3) = 12/54 = 2/9 percent ≈ 0.222222%.',r'As a proportion of the bloodstream, divide by 100: 1/450 ≈ 0.00222222. The key’s 0.22% = 0.0022 is a rounded version.',r'K(0) = 0 and K tends to zero as time grows, so the interior maximum is global for x ≥ 0.'],
 [r'Multiply gallons/hour by 32 hours and $2.25/gallon: C(x) = G(x)·32·2.25.',r'The multiplier 32·2.25/48 is 1.5, so C = 1.5(300/x + 2x) = 450/x + 3x dollars, x > 0.',r'Differentiate: C′ = −450/x² + 3. Set it to zero: x² = 150.',r'The only allowed root is x = √150 = 5√6 ≈ 12.24745, in the source’s thousand-kilowatt-hour units.',r'The second derivative C″ = 900/x³ > 0 establishes a minimum.',r'The cost grows without bound near zero and as x grows, making the interior minimum global.',r'C(√150) = 450/√150 + 3√150 = 6√150 = 30√6 ≈ $73.48469, or $73.48 to cents.']
]
ANSWERS=[r'(a) Critical −2,0; increasing outside [−2,0], decreasing (−2,0). (b) Critical −25,5/3; increasing (−∞,−25),(5/3,∞), decreasing (−25,5/3).',r'(a) −3,4; (b) −3,5; (c) 0,1; (d) 0,1.',r'(a) minimum at 1, maximum at −1, with the printed “= 0” inconsistency explained; (b) minimum at 1; (c) neither at 1, minimum at 2; (d) neither at 4 for the printed function.',r'(a) minimum (−18,−1), down left/up right, concave up everywhere. (b) maximum (−3,20), minimum (3,−16), inflection (0,2); full intervals above.',r'(a) maximum (0,1), minimum (2,−3); (b) maximum (−3,2), minimum (−1,−2).',r'(a) maximum at 2, minimum at 4, neither at −5,1. (b) maximum at −3, minimum at 1/2; 1 is a domain exclusion.',r'Advertising $6,000; maximum profit $51,200.',r'1,000,000 tires; maximum profit $700,000.',r'Increasing (0,3), decreasing (3,∞); maximum at 3 h of 2/9 percent ≈ 0.222222%.',r'x = 5√6 ≈ 12.24745 source units; minimum cost 30√6 ≈ $73.48.']
CLARIFICATIONS={0:'The key’s extra extrema-coordinate statements in part (b) are inconsistent with the source polynomial; derivative zeros and intervals are independently checked.',2:'Part (a) prints an extra = 0 although neither listed function value is zero. Part (d) prints ∛(x³ − 48), for which x = 4 is not critical; the key instead matches the different ∛(x³ − 48x). Source equations are retained.',5:'In part (b), the derivative pole at 1 cannot be made into a finite differentiable function point. It is excluded, rather than called a critical value with no extremum.',8:'The source concentration is measured in percent; the key rounds 2/9 percent to 0.22 percent. Exact percentage and dimensionless proportion are both provided.'}
